# 5. Database Design

## 5.1 `stories`

Purpose: Stores the logical story.

```sql
CREATE TABLE stories (
    id                  BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,
    title               VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    description         TEXT NULL,
    status              VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,
    assignment_enabled  BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT TRUE,
    allow_new_customers BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT TRUE,
    allow_after_completion BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT TRUE,
    assignment_weight   DECIMAL(10,4) NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,
    created_by          BIGINT NULL,
    updated_by          BIGINT NULL,
    created_at          TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,
    updated_at          TIMESTAMP NOT NULL
);
```

Recommended statuses:

```text
DRAFT
ACTIVE
PAUSED
ARCHIVED
```

Rules:

- `DRAFT`: never assigned.
- `ACTIVE`: eligible according to flags.
- `PAUSED`: do not assign new customers; existing assignments may continue depending on policy.
- `ARCHIVED`: never newly assigned.

Do not physically delete a story if it has historical deliveries.

---

## 5.2 `story_letters`

Purpose: Stores each ordered letter/chapter.

```sql
CREATE TABLE story_letters (
    id                    BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,
    story_id              BIGINT NOT NULL,
    sequence_number       INT NOT NULL,
    title                 VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    summary               TEXT NULL,
    continuity_summary    TEXT NULL,
    content               TEXT NOT NULL,
    status                VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'ACTIVE',
    created_at            TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,
    updated_at            TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,

    FOREIGN KEY (story_id) REFERENCES stories(id),

    UNIQUE (story_id, sequence_number)
);
```

`summary`:

- Internal/general summary.
- Useful for admin and reporting.

`continuity_summary`:

- Specifically written for natural reference from the next letter.
- Example:
  > I had just discovered a mysterious locked room inside the abandoned house.

`content`:

- Main story content.

Recommended status:

```text
DRAFT
ACTIVE
ARCHIVED
```

Do not assume `MAX(sequence_number)` is always the final letter if drafts exist. A published-story validation must explicitly determine the final active sequence.

---

## 5.3 `customer_story_assignments`

Purpose: Tracks which story a customer is currently progressing through.

```sql
CREATE TABLE customer_story_assignments (
    id                    BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,
    customer_id           BIGINT NOT NULL,
    story_id              BIGINT NOT NULL,

    status                VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,

    next_sequence_number  INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,

    started_at            TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,
    completed_at          TIMESTAMP NULL,

    created_at            TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,
    updated_at            TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,

    FOREIGN KEY (customer_id) REFERENCES customers(id),
    FOREIGN KEY (story_id) REFERENCES stories(id)
);
```

Recommended statuses:

```text
ACTIVE
COMPLETED
CANCELLED
```

Recommended constraint:

```text
At most one ACTIVE customer_story_assignment per customer
```

Implement using a partial/filtered unique index if supported, or enforce transactionally.

Example PostgreSQL:

```sql
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ux_customer_active_story
ON customer_story_assignments(customer_id)
WHERE status = 'ACTIVE';
```

