# 72. Final Engineering Principle

The most important separation in this system is:

```text
STORY PROGRESS
       ≠
LETTER TEMPLATE
       ≠
ORDER/MANUFACTURER
       ≠
RENDERED DOCUMENT
```

They interact, but they must not be coupled.

The authoritative sequence is:

```text
Customer
   ↓
Customer Story Assignment
   ↓
Story Letter Sequence
```

The presentation is:

```text
Customer + Order + Product + Story Context
   ↓
Template Selection
   ↓
Template Rendering
```

The historical record is:

```text
Letter Delivery
   ↓
Immutable Rendered Snapshot
```

This architecture ensures that:

- changing manufacturers does not reset the story;
- changing templates does not change the story sequence;
- changing story content does not rewrite historical letters;
- reprinting does not consume another letter;
- concurrent orders cannot accidentally receive the same letter;
- stories can have arbitrary lengths;
- new stories can be added without code changes;
- new templates can be added without code changes;
- customers can move automatically from one story to another;
- administrators retain full visibility and controlled override capability;
- manufacturers perform only the simple physical fulfillment action;
- existing platform functionality remains isolated from the new feature.

The allocation transaction and database constraints are the core correctness boundary. UI logic, random selection, caching, and rendering must never be allowed to override that boundary.
